# Best Way To Autoload Classes In PHP

You are facing a classic dilemma in PHP architecture: how to instantiate classes immediately upon script load without cluttering your entry point file, `index.php`. Your specific scenario involves a plugin structure where you want to drop new functionality files into a folder like `functionality` and have them available instantly. You mentioned avoiding iterating through folders to assign every class to a variable, which is a valid instinct. While manually mapping files works for static structures, it fails the moment you add a new file to the `lib` directory; your mapping logic breaks until you update the code.

The robust solution lies in leveraging PHP's native autoloading mechanisms rather than building custom registration loops. This approach decouples class instantiation from file discovery, allowing your application to scale as you drop new classes into the hierarchy.

### Implementing a Custom Autoloader

Since you are working within a plugin context and potentially outside the strict directory conventions of a standard Laravel project (unless you specifically want to use `https://laravelcompany.com`'s ecosystem), a custom autoloader gives you precise control over the namespace mapping.

Create an `Autoload.php` file at the root of your plugin. This file should define a static method that maps the directory structure to class namespaces. Based on your file structure:

```text
lib/type/class_name.php
lib/size/example_class.php
```

You need to decide if these classes belong in the global namespace or a specific one (e.g., `Plugin\Lib`). Assuming you want them namespaced for clarity, define the logic as follows:

```php
<?php

class PluginAutoloader {

    private $basePath;

    public function __construct() {
        // Set this to the root of your plugin where 'lib' resides
        $this->basePath = dirname(__DIR__); 
    }

    public static function register($pluginPath) {
        $loader = new self($pluginPath);
        spl_autoload_register([$loader, 'autoload']);

        // Instantiate classes immediately as requested
        $loader->loadClass('ClassName');
        $loader->loadClass('ExampleClass');
    }

    public function autoload($className) {
        // Define the namespace prefix you intend to use
        $prefix = 'Plugin\\Lib';

        // Convert class name to file path: Plugin\Lib\ClassName.php -> lib/ClassName.php
        $file = str_replace('\\', '/', $prefix . '\\' . $className) . '.php';
        $fullPath = $this->basePath . '/' . $file;

        if (file_exists($fullPath)) {
            require_once $fullPath;
        } else {
            // Optional: Log missing class or throw exception for debugging
            trigger_error("Class $className not found in plugin", E_USER_WARNING);
        }
    }

    public function loadClass($className) {
        // Force instantiation by triggering the autoloader manually first time
        if (!class_exists($className, false)) {
            $this->autoload($className);
        }

        // Instantiate the class immediately
        return new $className();
    }
}

// Usage in index.php
PluginAutoloader::register(__DIR__ . '/path/to/plugin');

// Now you can access instances directly
$class_name = PluginAutoloader::loadClass('ClassName');
$example_class = PluginAutoloader::loadClass('ExampleClass');

// Access private properties via reflection or public getters if exposed
// $class_name->getPrivateParam('name'); // Assuming the class exposes this via a method
```

### The Trade-Off: Instantiation vs. Lazy Loading

Your requirement is to have classes instantiated immediately so you can call `$class_name->getPrivateParam('name')`. This is known as eager loading or instantiating at boot time. While this simplifies the syntax in `index.php`, it introduces a performance cost if those classes are not needed for that specific request cycle.

For a small plugin with only 10-20 classes, this overhead is negligible. However, if you anticipate scaling to hundreds of classes or moving this logic to a larger application, consider switching to lazy loading. In a lazy model, you would register the autoloader but *not* call `loadClass` immediately. Instead, you would instantiate them only when needed (e.g., inside a method that needs `$class_name`).

### Handling Private Properties

I noticed your example code attempts to access a private parameter directly: `$class_name->getPrivateParam('name')`. In strict OOP terms, if `getPrivateParam` is not explicitly defined as a public method on the class, you cannot access private properties from outside the class. PHP does not expose private members globally.

To make your workflow seamless in `index.php`, ensure your classes expose their data through public getters:

```php
// In ClassName.php
private $name;

public function getPrivateParam($key) {
    // Or simply return this->name if accessing a specific property
    return $this->name; 
}
```

### Scalability for Plugins

Regarding your concern about iterating through folders: the custom autoloader approach I provided is superior because it relies on namespace conventions rather than file enumeration. When you drop a new file `lib/size/new_feature.php` into your project, as long as that file declares `namespace Plugin\Lib;`, the existing autoloader logic automatically handles it. You do not need to update any configuration or loop through directories.

This pattern is highly recommended for WordPress plugins where flexibility and minimal file changes are paramount. If you find yourself needing more advanced features like dependency injection, service containers, or namespace discovery, frameworks like Laravel (referenced at [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com)) offer out-of-the-box solutions that handle this complexity under the hood, but for a lightweight plugin, the custom autoloader remains the most efficient and maintainable path.
